    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 107.88, 596.078, 504.156, 626.885 ],
    "font" : "CMR9",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Abstract. We prove an inversion formula for the exterior k-plane transform. As a consequence, we show that if m < k then an m-current in Rn can be reconstructed from its projections onto Rk, which proves a conjecture of Solomon."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 506.193, 540.044, 568.942 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "A basic problem in analysis and geometry is to reconstruct an object in euclidean space from its orthogonal projections. Here we are interested in reconstructing an oriented submanifold, or more generally a current, from its projections. The geometry needed to prove our main theorem was worked out by Solomon [Sol11], who conjectured that the analysis would work out as well. In this brief note, we prove that conjecture."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 454.353, 540.054, 504.142 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Throughout, ﬁx integers 0 ≤ m < k < n. A compactly supported m-current is a continuous linear functional on the space of smooth m-forms [Sim84, §6.2]. If T is a compactly supported m-current and α is a smooth m-form, we write T α for T(α). Thus every compact, oriented, C1 submanifold of Rn is a compactly supported m-current."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 71.998, 415.593, 540.02, 454.178 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Let G be the Grassmannian variety of k-dimensional linear subspaces of Rn. There is a natural O(n)-action on G, which induces a unique O(n)-invariant Borel probability measure on G. For any P ∈ G, we have two operations:"
